  # Backfill: convert secrets already stored in PLAINTEXT to one-way digests.
  #
  # Why this is safe for EXISTING clients/tokens
  # --------------------------------------------
  # At migration time the plaintext is still present in each column, so we hash
  # it IN PLACE. Nothing needs to be re-issued: a client keeps working because it
  # still holds the original value (client_secret / refresh token / auth code)
  # and presents it as before — the gem now re-hashes what is presented and
  # matches it against the stored digest. Access-token JWTs are likewise matched
  # by digest for the revocation lookup; the client keeps sending the full JWT.
  #
  # The `sha256$` prefix makes each digest self-identifying, which:
  #   * makes this migration idempotent (a value already prefixed is skipped), and
  #   * disambiguates a digest from a plaintext SecureRandom.hex(32) — both are
  #     otherwise 64 hex characters.
  #
  # Hashing is done in Ruby (not a DB-specific SQL function) so it runs portably
  # on SQLite, MySQL, and PostgreSQL.
  #
  # Irreversible by design.
